{#
  Case study marquee for a static content page (the caseStudy single), as opposed to
  the one Jonson renders inside an answer (_components/case-studies.twig).

  Same markup and the same clients-marquee.js behaviour — fill, clone, drift, drag —
  but with the --static modifier, which full-bleeds off the viewport instead of
  breaking out with 100cqi. The Jonson version relies on .c-jonson's container-type
  for that, and a content page has no container.

  Expects: studies — a list of caseStudies entries, already ordered and filtered.
           stacked — true to render the column instead of the drifting strip. It is the
                     same layout the marquee already falls back to under reduced motion
                     and at narrow widths; this asks for it deliberately, at any width.

  Thumbnails load lazily. Unlike the Jonson marquee this strip is in normal document
  flow — at the foot of a case study, below everything — so the browser can be left to
  decide, and a visitor who never scrolls that far never pays for the images. The card
  defaults to eager for the Jonson case; this is the override.

  Cards carry `longTitle ?: title`, the same as everywhere else — one name per project
  — with the client as the eyebrow above it, read from the study's `clientSelector`
  relation rather than its Title. Those used to be the same thing; they aren't now that
  a client can have more than one study.
#}
{% set stacked = stacked ?? false %}
{% if studies is defined and studies %}
    <div class="c-case-studies c-case-studies--marquee c-case-studies--static{{ stacked ? ' c-case-studies--stacked' : '' }}"
         role="group" aria-label="Case studies">
        <div class="c-case-studies__track">
            <ul class="c-case-studies__items">
                {# NOT reversed, unlike the Jonson marquee next door.
                
                   It used to be, to cancel out a drift that ran left to right: cards
                   arrived at the LEFT edge, so the strip revealed the DOM backwards and
                   the loop was reversed to put the intended order back into it. The
                   cost was the frame that matters most — the visitor lands on the DOM's
                   FIRST cards, so the strip opened on the tail of the list, which is
                   the least relevant end of it now that the caller leads with the same
                   client's other work and the featured picks.
                
                   DIRECTION is 1 now (case-study-marquee.js), so the cards run right to
                   left and arrive at the RIGHT edge — the DOM reveals forwards on its
                   own and there's nothing left to cancel. Source order is the order in
                   both senses: what you land on, and what drifts in after it. #}
                {% for study in studies %}
                    {# The short client name: the eyebrow sits above the title in small
                       type, so the full "The White Paper Conference Company" form
                       belongs on the study's own page, not here. '' when there's no
                       client, which drops the line — the card still reads as itself. #}
                    {% set client = craft.frontend.caseStudyClientName(study) %}
                    {% set heading = (study.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: study.title|trim %}
                    {# The chip and the big thumbnail are the LEAD card's, not every
                       card's. From the second study on these tile two-up at 547 — half
                       the measure — and a 260px chip beside the text leaves about 205
                       for the title, which is the same arithmetic that keeps the logo
                       off the drifting slides next door. The 1110 image would be twice
                       the size those cards can show, too. #}
                    <li class="c-case-studies__item">
                        {% include '_components/case-study' with {
                            url: study.url,
                            title: heading,
                            client: client,
                            image: study.largeImage.one() ?? null,
                            loading: 'lazy',
                            logo: stacked and loop.first ? craft.frontend.caseStudyClientLogo(study) : null,
                            restTitle: true,
                            imageWidths: stacked and loop.first ? [1110, 2220] : [547, 1094],
                            imageSizes: stacked and loop.first ? '(min-width: 80em) 1110px, 100vw' : imageSizes ?? null,
                            imageFallback: stacked and loop.first ? 1110 : null,
                        } only %}
                    </li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
{% endif %}
